<p>
  You do not need an EsignCenter account to sign something. Somebody sent you
  a document, and this page describes exactly what you will see, in order.
</p>

<h2>Opening your document</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Open the email invitation and choose the button in it, or open the link you were given. The link is yours alone.</li>
  <li>If the sender asked for email verification, we email you a one-time code first. Type it in (or use the link in that email) and you are through. It is there so that only the person holding your inbox can open the document.</li>
  <li>The document opens in your browser &mdash; phone, tablet or computer, nothing to install.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Filling it in</h2>

<p>
  The fields the sender placed for you are highlighted, and the page walks you
  from one to the next. Depending on the document you may type text, pick a
  date, tick a box, choose from a list, attach a file, or draw, type or upload
  your signature and initials. Anything marked required has to be filled in
  before you can finish.
</p>

<h2>Agreeing to sign electronically</h2>

<p>
  Above the buttons is a checkbox: <em>I agree to use electronic records and
  signatures.</em> You cannot complete the document until it is ticked, and two
  things sit beside it.
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>View this document as a PDF</strong> opens the unsigned original in a new tab. The checkbox stays locked until you have used it once &mdash; the point is that you prove to yourself your device can open a PDF before you agree to be sent one.</li>
  <li><strong>Electronic Signature Disclosure</strong> opens the full notice: who sent this document and the email address that reaches them, what you need to read and keep a copy, how to ask for a paper copy and who may charge for it, how to change the address your documents go to, and what we record.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  Your agreement is recorded once, with the date and time, your network
  address, the language you read the notice in and a fingerprint of the exact
  wording, and it is printed in the audit trail. You are not asked again on
  later steps.
</p>

<aside class="help-callout">
  <p>
    <strong>Good to know:</strong> you can withdraw before you finish simply by
    not signing &mdash; tell the sender at the address named in the disclosure,
    free of charge, and it is then for them to arrange a paper copy. That
    address is the sender's, not ours: they own the document and the paperwork
    around it.
  </p>
</aside>

<h2>If you should not be the one signing</h2>

<p>
  Where the sender has allowed it, two buttons are offered.
  <strong>Decline</strong> stops the document and lets you send the sender a
  reason. <strong>Delegate</strong> hands the document to somebody else by
  email &mdash; they get a fresh link, and because they are a different person
  they tick the consent box for themselves.
</p>

<h2>After you sign</h2>

<p>
  You can download your copy straight away, and we email it to you when
  everybody has finished. The finished PDF is digitally sealed and timestamped,
  with an audit trail attached. Keep it: anyone can later check it on our
  <%= link_to 'verify page', verify_path %>, which is covered in
  <%= link_to 'Verify a signed document', help_article_path('verify-a-signed-document') %>.
</p>

<p>
  Questions about the contents of the document belong with whoever sent it.
  Problems with the page itself belong with
  <%= link_to 'us', support_path %>.
</p>
